  {
   "id": "noteIntro",
   "type": "NoteNode",
   "x": -40,
   "y": -200,
   "init": {
    "label": "A tiny transformer",
    "color": "violet",
    "collapsed": false,
    "width": 820,
    "height": 560,
    "body": "# A tiny transformer\n\nThe forward pass of a language model, the same arithmetic as GPT at toy size: one layer, one attention head, four numbers per word and a vocabulary of five. Read it left to right; each step is a card, and each stage ends in a picture of what it made.\n\n1. **Read the prompt.** Each word of *the cat sat on* is found in the vocabulary, which is in alphabetical order: *the* is word 5, *cat* word 1.\n2. **Embed.** Each word's number picks its row of four numbers out of the embedding table, and each position adds its own. The heatmap shows the table: words used the same way get similar rows, so *cat* and *mat* look alike. Here the four numbers were set by hand to mean article, noun, verb and preposition; a real model learns hundreds of numbers per word, and they rarely mean anything so tidy.\n3. **Attend.** Each word asks with its query and answers with its key. A query times a key, divided by 2 (the square root of the 4 dimensions, which keeps the scores from growing with the size), scores how much one word should look at another; the mask hides the words that come after, and a softmax turns each row into weights that sum to 1 (both Softmax cards are one small graph: Edit Contents shows its steps, ending in a matrix multiply that scales each row by one over its total). The weights mix the words themselves (a full model first passes them through a third matrix, W_V, which here would change nothing), and the mix is added back. Here *sat* and *on* put nearly all their attention on *cat*.\n4. **Think.** A small network with one hidden unit and a ReLU works on each word by itself, and its answer is added back too.\n5. **Predict.** The unembedding scores every word of the vocabulary, and a softmax makes the scores probabilities. Each position predicts the word after it: *the* expects *cat* or *mat*, *cat* expects *sat*, *sat* expects *on*, and *on* expects *the*.\n\nThe weights were set by hand to tell that story; a real model learns billions of them from text. Two things to try: shorten the prompt to *the cat sat* and watch *on* become the next word, or set the 3 in W_K's second row to 0 and watch the attention on *cat* spread out.",
    "fieldTypes": {}
   }
  },
